{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And you shall gird them with sashes</b> — this hints at Aaron and his sons, and it returns to specify Aaron and his sons, like \"he who brings the offering of YHWH\" (Exodus 35:24), \"who would not serve him, Nebuchadnezzar\" (Jeremiah 27:8), according to the words of Rabbi Abraham. If so, \"and you shall bind caps on them\" (Exodus 29:9) reverts to some of them, for Aaron has no cap, and the mitre is already on his head. And it is possible that the proper reading of the verse is: \"And you shall gird them, a sash, Aaron and his sons\" — that is to say, you shall gird his sons, the ones mentioned, with a sash, and Aaron himself together with his sons. And since the sash is identical for all of them (Yoma 12a), it was not mentioned together with Aaron's garments above (Exodus 29:5–6), and it was necessary now to state that he should gird the sash like his sons. And the breeches were not mentioned, because there was no need, as I have explained (Ramban on Exodus 28:35). And the reason that the breeches were separated from the rest of the garments is that all the garments Moshe would put on them, as he was commanded (Exodus 29:8), \"And you shall dress them,\" but the breeches, which are to cover the flesh of nakedness (Exodus 28:42), they themselves would put on in modesty; therefore he did not mention them now in the general statement \"and you shall take the garments and dress them\" (Exodus 29:8), and consequently he set them apart both in the commandment and in the penalty, as I have noted above (Exodus 28:42)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:18:03.845470574Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}